# Git Commit Guidelines

### Git Commit Rules

#### When to Check These Rules
- Before committing changes
- When writing commit messages
- When reviewing commits
- When preparing for pull requests

#### Rules
##### Message Format
- Format: `[Verb] [Brief description]` for each distinct action
- Standard Verbs: Add, Fix, Update, Remove, Refactor
- Description: Clear, concise, present tense
- Use git diff to determine the changes
- Each file change or major functional change should have its own action message
- Multiple actions should be combined into a single line using commas
- Order actions by their impact (major changes first)

##### Command Rules
- Non-destructive read-only commands (git status, cat, ls, etc.) can be executed automatically
- Any command that modifies files, stages changes, or commits requires explicit approval
- Always explain the purpose of the command before executing it
- Always add all modified files when committing changes
- Never selectively add files unless explicitly requested
- Use `git add .` as the default command for staging changes

#### Examples
✅ Correct Messages:
```
[Add] Login feature, password reset functionality
[Fix] Button alignment in login form
[Refactor] Authentication service to use JWT
[Update] User profile form validation
[Remove] Deprecated API endpoint
```

✅ Multiple Actions:
```
[Add] New user profile page, profile image upload functionality
[Refactor] User model to support profile images
[Update] User controller to handle image uploads
[Fix] Image validation in profile form
[Remove] Old profile image storage method
```

❌ Incorrect:
- "Fixed stuff"
- "Updated things"
- "Changes"
- "WIP"
- "More changes"
- "Fix security issue" (too vague)
- Using newlines in commit messages
- Using \n for line breaks

#### Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Vague commit messages
- Multiple unrelated changes in one commit
- Not following the message format
- Not explaining command purpose
- Selective file addition without reason

#### Validation Checklist
- [ ] Message follows format
- [ ] Changes are properly described
- [ ] Multiple actions are combined correctly
- [ ] Commands are explained
- [ ] All files are added
- [ ] No sensitive data included
